// This file is the *reasoning*: what each setting is for, what it cost to
// get right, and which ones are opinions. The values themselves come out of
// the code -- `sl0ppty --dump-config` writes every knob with the value it
// actually holds, and `C-a e` seeds a missing config with exactly that.
//
// So if the two ever disagree, the dump is right and this file is stale;
// tests/test_dump_config.py fails when a setting exists and is not rendered.
//
// ---- what reloads ----
//
// Saving this file applies it to every running session, and nearly everything
// here takes effect on the next frame: colours, geometry, the status bar and
// line, the marks, shader chains and their states, hints, and the keys --
// including the leader itself.
//
// The exceptions, both because of *when* they are read rather than any
// reluctance to reload:
//
//   shell        consulted when a pane is made, so it applies to the next
//                pane you open rather than to the ones already running
//   shader_dir   plugins are loaded on reload but never unloaded, so a new
//                .so is picked up and a replaced one needs a new session
//
// A session started before this file existed still notices it appearing: the
// watch is on the directory, and sl0ppty creates that directory at startup so
// there is always something to watch.
//
// sl0ppty — every value here is also the compiled-in default, so this file is
// documentation as much as configuration. Copy it to
// ~/.config/sl0ppty/config.kdl (or point $SL0PPTY_CONFIG at it) and edit.
//
// A broken file costs a warning on stderr and nothing else: the defaults
// stand. Saving this file re-reads it into every running session on its own;
// `sl0ppty --no-reload` starts one that does not watch, and
// `sl0ppty cmd '{"cmd":"reload"}'` asks for a re-read explicitly. And
// refuses a file it cannot parse rather than half-applying it.

// ── include ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A config can be made of files:
//
//     include "themes/amber.kdl"        // contrib/themes has six
//     include "keys/vim.kdl" "shaders/crt.kdl"
//
// which is how you switch a theme by editing one line instead of pasting one.
// Nothing about it is theme-specific: an include is another config file applied
// here, so the same line composes keys, shader chains, states or geometry.
//
// A relative path is relative to *the file doing the including*, never to the
// directory you happened to start the session from -- a config is a thing on
// disk that refers to its neighbours. A leading `~` is your home directory, and
// an absolute path is itself. Included files may include others.
//
// What you include is the *base*: the file with the `include` line in it wins
// wherever they disagree, and a later include wins over an earlier one. That is
// true wherever the line sits in the file, because the loader reads a document
// by asking it for the keys it knows rather than walking it top to bottom -- so
// "here" is not a position it could honour, and "the file you are editing wins"
// is the rule worth having anyway.
//
// `keys` blocks *add* to what came before, like every keys block; a `shaders`
// or `states` block replaces the one it inherited, like every one of those.
//
// A file that will not load is skipped and the rest of the config still
// applies -- losing your keybindings over a mistyped theme name would be the
// worse answer -- and the session says which file it could not honour, in the
// same place it says "config reloaded". That goes for anything a config
// complains about: an unknown shader, a binding that does not parse, an include
// that is not there. The file applied; one line of it did not; you get told.

// ── geometry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Blank cells between panes and around the screen edge, in ROWS.
gap 1

// Columns per row. Terminal cells are about twice as tall as they are wide, so
// a gap of 1 row and 2 columns looks square.
gap_aspect 2

// Cells between a pane's frame and its content.
padding 0

// ╭╮╰╯ versus ┌┐└┘
rounded true

// "left" | "center" | "right"
title_align "center"

// Cells between a frame's corner and the start of its title, for the edge the
// title is anchored to. The title always carries a space on each side on top
// of this, so it can never end up welded to the rule beside it.
title_inset 2

// ── the bell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A BEL from a program marks the pane it came from, and the tab that pane is
// in -- a bell exists for the pane you are *not* looking at, and one that only
// showed on screen would be useless in a background tab. Looking at the pane
// is what puts it out. Off makes a bell silent *and* invisible, which is a
// real choice rather than an oversight.
//
// The mark is the quiet default. A louder one is a shader rather than a knob:
// `bell` is a pane state, so `states { bell { ... } }` colours or flashes a
// rung pane's frame for as long as the bell is unanswered. See the chrome
// passes further down.
// ── the frame's own buttons ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Minimise, zoom and close, in the top-right of every pane's frame: the mark
// and one space each, seven columns for the three of them. They are drawn
// right to left and stop when they run out of room, so a narrow pane keeps
// close and zoom and loses minimise first.
//
// The split button was removed from this frame once already, on the grounds
// that one glyph cannot mean four directions and the border can. These are two
// verbs a single glyph *can* mean, which is why they are here and splitting is
// still done by clicking an edge. They do cost six columns of every frame, so
// this turns them off -- the keybindings (C-a z, C-a x) are unaffected, because
// the setting is about the affordance and not the verb.
// A word in the middle of the status line for whatever the pointer is on:
// which way a border would split, what a one-character button does, that the
// gap between two panes can be dragged. The frame's affordances are mostly one
// character wide and a border that splits when clicked looks exactly like a
// border, so this is where they say so.
//
// It sits in the middle of the *row*, not in the space the two ends leave
// over: centred in the gap it moved every time a pane title changed length,
// and a thing you glance at should be in the same place twice. When the row is
// too full for it there it is not drawn at all -- the ends say which session
// and pane you are in, and a hint you can get back by hovering is the part
// that can afford to disappear.
hints true

// When there is no hint to show — which is most of the time, since a hint
// needs the pointer to be on something — that space says which sl0ppty this
// is: `sl0ppty 0.1.0+d9dee7f`. It reads as decoration and is not: a session
// keeps the binary it started with, so after a rebuild the version on screen
// is the one you are actually talking to, and `sl0ppty --version` is the one
// on disk. When those disagree, that is the answer to whatever you were about
// to debug. (contrib/sl0ppty-dev restarts into the new one.)
//
// It gives way to a hint, and both give way to the ends of the line -- they
// are centred on the row and simply not drawn when that would overlap.
version_banner true

pane_buttons true

// Four shapes, one verb each: down for away, an outline for "make it fill the
// tab", the same outline filled in for "it is filling it", a cross for gone.
//
// Deliberately not one tidy family. A set of boxed operators (⊞ ⊟ ⊡ ⊠) was
// tried and read as four identical smudges: they are *math* symbols, sized to
// sit beside x and y in an equation, so at a terminal's cell size the interior
// carrying the whole meaning is two or three pixels of it. Geometric shapes and
// dingbats are drawn at full cell size, which is what makes them readable at
// all, and picking glyphs that fill their cell is also the answer to the
// evenness that put plain ASCII here originally -- a glyph that does not fill
// its cell hands the slack to the gap beside it, by an amount the font decides.
//
// Single width, and never emoji: many terminals draw those two columns wide
// while chrome here is booked as one, which shifts everything after them.
//
// A mark may be more than one character; the button is measured rather than
// assumed, so "[]" and "][" work if you prefer them. Put the character itself
// here; this file has no \u escape.
zoom_mark "□"          // click to fill the tab with this pane
zoom_on_mark "■"       // ...and this is what it says while it is filling it
close_mark "✕"
min_mark "▼"           // into the strip along the bottom, still running

// The new-tab button at the end of the tab strip. Drawn with a space on each
// side, so a one-character mark is still a three-cell target -- a one-cell
// target is a thing you miss with a mouse. It reads as a bare "+" because
// nothing else in the UI is one: the frame's old "+" for splitting went when
// the border became the button. Try "[+]" or "new" if you prefer a word.
newtab_mark "+"

// Which dead panes stay, and which just go.
//
//   commands   panes that were *given* a command -- from a layout, or the
//              control API. Default. Their death is news: the pane keeps what
//              it printed, says in its own backlog why it ended, and offers
//              [re-run] and [close] in its frame.
//   all        shells too. A pane you split off and typed `exit` in stays
//              until you dismiss it.
//   none       nothing stays; the pane is closed on the next paint, and with
//              the last pane, the session.
//
// The line is the command, not who made the pane: a shell is a shell whether
// it came from a layout or from C-a \, and when you type `exit` in one you
// are done with it. A pane running `npm run dev` is a different thing -- you
// want the error it left, and the button that runs it again.
//
// (`true` and `false` are still read, as `all` and `none`, because they used
// to be the spelling.)
keep_dead "commands"

// How far the panes you are *not* in are pushed back, 0..255, and 0 for not
// at all. On by default and gently: at 60 white text lands on #c3c3c3, which
// reads as "not this one" without reading as "not available", because half
// the time you are still reading the other pane.
//
// This is the one taste shipped as a default, and it is shipped because
// turning it off is a single obvious number rather than a shader incantation.
// Writing the chain yourself still wins -- `states { unfocused { ... } }`
// replaces it, and `states { unfocused { } }` means nothing at all.
dim_unfocused 60

bell_indicator true

// What to draw. A short string rather than a fixed glyph, because the obvious
// choice is an emoji bell and emoji are drawn two columns wide by many
// terminals while chrome here is booked as one -- so the default is narrow and
// anyone who knows their own terminal can say otherwise. (This file has no
// \u escape, so put the character itself here.)
bell_mark "•"

// The one-row bar with tabs, the new-tab button and the pane count.
status_bar true

// The line along the bottom: which session and tab you are in, what the
// focused pane calls itself, and whether it is in a state worth knowing about
// - scrolled back, on an alternate screen, or not started yet. On the right it
// says which of *this tab's* panes is open, as `pane 2/5`; the strip above
// counts the whole session, so the two disagree as soon as you have two tabs.
status_line true

// How far the strip and the line are held off the left and right edges. This
// is separate from `gap`, which is the space around the panes, and wider than
// it by default so the two bars read as chrome outside the layout rather than
// as another row of it.
status_pad 4

// Focus follows the pointer. It never steals focus mid-chord (while the prefix
// is held), never reaches past the finder, never interrupts a drag, and never
// expands a collapsed pane just because the pointer crossed its header.
focus_follows_mouse true

// The floor below which a pane is not small but useless. A split that cannot
// give every child this much collapses into a stack instead, with one pane
// expanded and its siblings as one-row headers. Recomputed every frame, so
// widening the terminal always restores exactly the layout you had.
min_pane cols=24 rows=6

// The smallest pane a split is allowed to *produce*. min_pane above is where
// the layout gives up and collapses; this is where splitting stopped being
// worth offering, which is a larger number and a different question — two
// twenty-five column panes technically fit and are not worth having.
//
// Below this the border stops being a button: no guide arms, clicking it does
// nothing, and the keyboard says so with a toast. The control API and layout
// files are deliberately exempt, because a script asking for a pane is saying
// what it wants rather than being offered something, and the responsive
// collapse is what catches it there.
//
// Never used below min_pane: refusing to offer what the layout would collapse
// anyway is the floor under the floor. Raise it if you like fewer, bigger
// panes; a column split needs 2*cols + 2 columns of pane to be offered, a row
// split 2*rows + 1.
min_split cols=32 rows=8

// Rows per wheel notch, and per scroll-up/scroll-down.
scroll_lines 3

// How long the pointer must rest on a pane border before the split guide
// arms. Contact alone is not enough: a pointer crossing a border on its way
// somewhere else should not make the screen flash. 0 arms immediately.
hover_delay_ms 250

// How close together two clicks on a pane's title must be to count as a
// double-click, which renames that pane in place. Enter keeps the new name,
// Escape abandons it, and an empty name gives the pane back to whatever its
// program calls itself.
double_click_ms 400

// How far everything behind a modal (the cheatsheet, C-a ?) is pushed back,
// 0..255, and 0 for not at all. A modal floating over a screen as bright as
// it is reads as another pane rather than as something in front.
//
// It is the shader pass aimed at the whole screen, so the same caveat applies
// as for shaders: cells with no colour of their own are resolved through
// theme's default_fg/default_bg first, and if those do not match your
// terminal's real colours the screen will shift slightly while a modal is up.
modal_scrim 120

// Extra shaders can be loaded from shared libraries: any `*.so` in the
// directory below that exports a sl0ppty shader plugin. Once loaded they are
// named here exactly like a built-in. Default: a `shaders` directory beside
// this file, so dropping the .so next to your config is the whole install.
//
// A plugin is native code running in the session's own process, with your
// privileges and no sandbox -- which is the same thing `shell` and a layout's
// `command=` already are, and the reason the search path is a directory you
// own rather than a value that can point anywhere. Install ones you trust.
//
// Adding a plugin and saving this file loads it; nothing is ever unloaded, so
// *replacing* one needs a new session. Skeleton and Makefile:
// contrib/shader-plugin/
//
// shader_dir "~/.config/sl0ppty/shaders"

// ── shaders ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Colour passes over a pane's contents (never the frame, title or tab strip).
// Both are strengths from 0 to 255, where 0 is off and 255 is fully applied.
// Both resolve default-coloured text through theme's default_fg/default_bg,
// so set those to your terminal's real colours or a shaded pane will shift.

// What a pane looks like while it is in a particular state. Each state takes
// the same chain of passes `shaders` does.
//
// A pane is usually in several states at once — unfocused *and* scrolled,
// suspended *and* something you could drop onto — so exactly one wins: the
// first that matches, tested in this order.
//
//   dragging     the pane you have hold of
//   drop_hover   the one under the pointer, where it would land
//   drop_target  the others, all of them somewhere it could go
//   dead         its program exited; waiting to be re-run or closed
//   suspended    laid out, never started (its label is its contents)
//   bell         a BEL arrived and nobody has looked since; ends when you do
//   scrolled     looking at scrollback rather than at the present
//   unfocused    the weakest thing that can be true of a pane
//
// The order is fixed rather than read from this block, because it is a ranking
// by urgency and not a preference: a pane you are holding should not be
// recoloured by anything, and a mode the whole screen is in outranks an
// ambient hint about one pane. They do not stack, for the same reason two
// reasons to be grey would compound into one muddy grey that reads as neither.
//
// Naming a state replaces its default outright — including with nothing, which
// is how you turn one off:  states { drop_target { } }
//
// Which states have a default, and why the line falls where it does:
//
//   a pane that is *not live* gets one -- dead, suspended, scrolled. In all
//   three the cells are showing something other than a running program's
//   present: output from something that exited, a pane that never started, or
//   the past. None of that is discoverable by looking unless something says
//   so, which is the entire argument for colouring it.
//
//   a pane that is merely *not the one you are in* is a taste rather than a
//   fact, and gets a default anyway -- but through `dim_unfocused` above,
//   which is one number to change or zero out. The objection to shipping a
//   taste was never the taste, it was that turning it off should not require
//   knowing this block exists. `dragging` still has no default: the pane in
//   your hand keeping its colour is what makes it lift off the page.
//
// All the defaults are gentle: a dead pane, a suspended one and scrollback are
// all things you still want to read. The compiled-in scrolled tint uses the
// scrollback indicator's own colour (theme's scroll_bg) so a theme moves both
// together; written here it is spelled out, because a `color=` you do not give
// falls back to theme's frame_focus, which is a different setting that happens
// to hold the same value in every theme we ship.
states {
    drop_hover { grayscale amount=200; dim amount=140 }
    drop_target { grayscale amount=200; dim amount=140 }
    dead { grayscale amount=200; dim amount=90 }
    suspended { grayscale amount=170; dim amount=60 }
    scrolled { tint amount=22 color="#ff5fd7" }   // = theme's scroll_bg
    // dragging { }
    // bell { }    // see the chrome passes below for a border flash
    // unfocused { dim amount=60 }    // = dim_unfocused above; yours replaces it
}

// While a pane is being dragged its border stays solid and every other pane's
// turns dashed, marking them as places it could be dropped. That is chrome,
// not a shader, so it happens whatever the states above are set to.

// Colour passes every pane gets, in the order written — a chain is a sequence,
// so `grayscale` then `tint` is not `tint` then `grayscale`. Each node is one
// pass; `amount` is 0..255 and defaults to 128. Listing a shader twice is how
// you get two of it (two rulers, say). An unknown name is refused with a
// warning and skipped, never guessed at.
//
//   dim        amount                    darken towards black
//   grayscale  amount                    drain colour, weighted for the eye
//   tint       amount color="#rrggbb"    pull towards a colour
//   vignette   amount                    darken towards the pane's edges
//   gradient   amount direction=0..3     fade towards the background
//                                        0 down, 1 up, 2 right, 3 left
//   zebra      amount band=1             darken alternate bands of `band` rows;
//                                        band=1 is also a CRT scanline
//   ruler      amount at=80 color=       mark a column, background only, so the
//                                        text on it stays readable
//   margin     amount at=100             everything from that column recedes
//   spotlight  amount radius=12          brightness falls away from the cursor
//
// Two props are the *pass's* rather than the shader's, and work on any of them:
//
//   where="content"|"chrome"   the contents, or the frame (see further down)
//   channel="both"|"fg"|"bg"   which of the cell's two colours it may keep;
//                              `fg` recolours glyphs and leaves backgrounds
//                              alone, which is what a border wants
//
// `amount` can also be an *expression* instead of a number, evaluated for
// every cell. That is how you get an effect nobody built in, without building
// anything:
//
//     shaders {
//         dim  amount="(y % 2) * 40"                       // scanlines
//         dim  amount="(x > cols - 10) * 120"              // the right margin
//         tint amount="255 - dist(x, y, curx, cury) * 12" color="#ff5fd7"
//     }
//
// The expression produces the *strength*, never the colour: the mixing stays
// in C, so an expression cannot make an invalid cell, and its result is one
// byte per cell -- which is what lets the whole program be evaluated once into
// a per-cell map and then reused. A user-written positional shader therefore
// costs about what a compiled one does. One that reads the clock cannot be
// cached and is evaluated per cell per frame, which is the price of animation.
//
//   variables   x y            the cell, inside the pane's content rect
//               cols rows      the size of that rect
//               curx cury      the cursor, and `cursor` (0 or 1) for whether
//                              this pane has it
//               focused        0 or 1
//               t              milliseconds, for animation
//   operators   + - * / %      integer only; division by zero is 0, not a crash
//               < > <= >= == != and && || !, which give 0 or 1 -- so
//                              `(x < 10) * 200` is how you write a rule
//               ? :            both sides are evaluated, then one is chosen
//   functions   min(a,b) max(a,b) abs(a) clamp(v,lo,hi)
//               dist(x1,y1,x2,y2)  counts a row double, because a cell is
//                                  about twice as tall as it is wide
//
// There are no loops and no recursion, on purpose: a config cannot spin, and
// there is nothing to sandbox. An expression that does not compile drops that
// one shader with a warning -- never the config, and never a half-strength
// version of what you asked for.
//
// Empty by default: these are yours to ask for, not opinions we hold.
//
// shaders {
//     vignette amount=70
//     zebra amount=22
//     ruler amount=60 at=80 color="#ff5fd7"
//     spotlight amount=90 radius=14
// }

// ── chrome passes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `where="chrome"` on any entry above runs that pass over a pane's *frame*
// instead of its contents: the border, the padding inside it, the title and
// the buttons. Everything else is identical -- same shaders, same `amount`,
// same expressions -- because a frame is just another rect, so it is just
// another pass. `where="content"` is the default and never has to be written.
//
// The two never touch each other's cells: the contents are cut out of the
// frame pass, so a full-strength chrome tint leaves your text alone and a
// full-strength content tint leaves the border alone.
//
// `channel="fg"` (or `"bg"`, or `"both"`, the default) says which of a cell's
// two colours a pass may keep. On a frame you almost always want `fg`: a
// border's *background* is the terminal's own default, and mixing that towards
// a colour paints a rectangle behind the glyphs instead of colouring them --
// and leaves it there until the pane changes state. `fg` also keeps the padding
// inside the border out of it, since a blank cell has no foreground to colour,
// so the effect lands on exactly the rules, the title and the buttons.
//
// `x`/`y` are the *whole frame's*, counted from its top-left corner, with
// `cols`/`rows` the size of the pane's rect -- not of one side. That is what
// lets an effect travel round a border rather than restart at every corner:
//
//     shaders {
//         // the top rule only, where the title is
//         tint where="chrome" channel="fg" color="#ff5fd7" amount="(y==0)*255"
//         // a corner glow. One node per line: a newline ends a node, so a
//         // pass split over two lines is two broken nodes, not one pass.
//         tint where="chrome" channel="fg" color="#00ffd7" amount="255-dist(x,y,0,0)*20"
//     }
//
// For anything that *travels*, though, `x`/`y` are rarely what you want: it
// wants distance *along the ring*, which the language can work
// out -- clockwise from the top-left corner, in cells:
//
//     (y == 0 ? x : x == cols - 1 ? cols - 1 + y
//      : y == rows - 1 ? 2 * cols + rows - 3 - x : 2 * cols + 2 * rows - 4 - y)
//
// with the perimeter itself `2 * cols + 2 * rows - 4`. Two things fall out of
// that: `min(d, P - d)` is distance the short way round, so an effect crosses
// the corner it started from instead of stopping at a seam; and
// `(p - head + P) % P` is "how far ahead of the head", which makes an arc
// rotate rather than a blob slide. There is no way to name a subexpression, so
// it has to be written out in full -- which is why a small effect is a long
// line. contrib/chrome has seven of them ready to paste (a shine running round
// the border, sparkles, ripples from a corner, a rotating arc, a static
// gradient, corner glints, a sheen across the title rule).
//
// Hung off a state, this is a border that says what a pane is doing -- which
// is what chrome is for, and it costs no room and no text:
//
//     states {
//         // a dead pane's frame breathes red until you deal with it: a
//         // triangle wave, up and back down over about four seconds
//         dead { tint where="chrome" channel="fg" color="#ff0033" amount="abs(t/8%510-255)" }
//         // a bell nobody has answered yet: `since` is how long the pane has
//         // been in this state, so this is a real *flash* -- full strength for
//         // a quarter second and then gone, without the bell being answered.
//         // `t` could not say that: it is the time, not how long ago.
//         bell { tint where="chrome" channel="fg" color="#ffcc00" amount="(since<250)*255" }
//         // one that has not started yet is outlined, not filled
//         suspended { tint where="chrome" channel="fg" color="#8a8a95" amount=160 }
//         // the panes you are not in recede, frame and contents together.
//         // Both passes are written out because naming a state replaces the
//         // *whole* state: a block holding only chrome passes is a state with
//         // no content pass, which for `unfocused` means dim_unfocused above
//         // stops applying to it.
//         unfocused { dim amount=60; dim where="chrome" amount=90 }
//     }
//
// A pane a small window has collapsed to a single row (see min_pane) is chrome
// all the way through, so a chrome pass is the only kind that can still colour
// it -- which is the state where a colour is worth the most.
//
// Empty by default, like the content passes: yours to ask for.

// How often to repaint while a shader is animated, in milliseconds -- which
// means one whose `amount` reads `t`. Nothing else needs a frame clock: every
// other repaint has an event behind it, and this one is only asked for while
// such a shader is actually on screen. 0 turns it off, which leaves an
// animated shader advancing only when something else causes a frame.
anim_ms 50

// How long an announcement ("copied 13 chars", a pane's OSC 9 notification,
// "config reloaded") stays in the bottom-right corner.
toast_ms 2500

// What a new pane runs. Unset means $SHELL.
// shell "/bin/bash"

// ── theme ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Every colour the compositor draws has its own name here. They started as six
// names doing thirty jobs, which meant the split guide could not be recoloured
// without also recolouring the focused frame, because they were the same
// entry. Several still share a default: that says they look right together,
// not that they are the same thing.
theme {
    // What a cell's "terminal default" colour is taken to be when a shader has
    // to compute on it. See the shaders section above.
    default_fg "#ffffff"
    default_bg "#000000"

    // ── panes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    frame_focus "#ff5fd7"      // border of the focused pane
    frame_idle "#45454a"       // border of every other pane
    title "#ffffff"            // its title, and its OSC 5577 status text
    title_idle "#45454a"       // the same, on a pane that is not focused

    // A pane's own OSC 5577 buttons.
    button_fg "#141418"
    button_bg "#ff5fd7"
    button_bg_idle "#55555c"   // buttons on a pane that is not focused

    // ── things that appear under the pointer ────────────────────────────────
    guide "#ff5fd7"            // split guide: armed edge, dashed boundary, arrow
    resize "#ff5fd7"           // the handle in the gap between two panes
    drop_target "#ff5fd7"      // frame of a pane a drag could land on

    // ── a pane's scrollback indicator ───────────────────────────────────────
    scroll_fg "#141418"
    scroll_bg "#ff5fd7"

    // ── rows of a tab that has collapsed into a list ────────────────────────
    header "#45454a"
    header_hover "#ff5fd7"          // the rule, under the pointer
    header_hover_title "#ffffff"    // the name, under the pointer

    // ── the strip along the top ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    // The tab you are in is filled rather than merely bold: bold alone is easy
    // to miss on a strip of short labels. It keeps that fill while the pointer
    // is on it -- it is still the tab you are in -- so its hover lands on the
    // text instead.
    tab_active_fg "#141418"
    tab_active_bg "#ff5fd7"
    tab_active_hover_fg "#ffffff"
    tab_idle "#45454a"
    tab_hover "#ff5fd7"
    prefix_fg "#141418"        // the "C-a" badge, while the prefix is pending
    prefix_bg "#ff5fd7"
    tab_count "#45454a"        // panes in the whole session

    // ── the line along the bottom ───────────────────────────────────────────
    status "#45454a"           // session, tab, pane, and the pane index
    status_state "#ffffff"     // scrolled / alt screen / not started / exited
    hint "#ffffff"             // what the pointer is on, in the middle

    // ── marks on a pane's frame ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    bell "#ff5fd7"             // a pane rang and has not been looked at
    dead "#ff875f"             // its program exited: the line in its backlog,
                               // and the words in its frame and the status line
    pane_button "#45454a"      // the frame's own close/zoom/minimise marks
    pane_button_hover "#ff5fd7"

    // ── the row of minimised panes ──────────────────────────────────────────
    minbar "#45454a"
    minbar_hover "#ff5fd7"

    // ── modals: a surface that floats over the layout ───────────────────────
    // Named apart from the pane colours they resemble because a modal is a
    // different surface: opaque, on top of everything, and legible against a
    // background that has been dimmed underneath it. (The first version drew
    // the close button in the pane button colour on the finder background,
    // which are the same value — an invisible button until you hovered it.)
    modal_bg "#1c1c22"          // a shade lighter than the dimmed screen
    modal_fg "#e8e8ea"
    modal_border "#ff5fd7"
    modal_title "#ffffff"
    modal_button "#8a8a95"      // visible *without* the pointer on it
    modal_button_hover "#ff5fd7"

    // ── the pane finder ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // The finder is a modal, so its box is the modal colours above; these are
    // the parts only it has. `finder_bg` is the query field and the row under
    // the pointer — a band that is not the box, so typing and hovering both
    // have somewhere to show.
    finder_fg "#ffffff"
    finder_bg "#45454a"
    finder_sel_fg "#141418"
    finder_sel_bg "#ff5fd7"      // the row enter would take

    // ── the in-place editor a double-click opens on a pane title ────────────
    rename_fg "#141418"
    rename_bg "#ff5fd7"

    // ── announcements ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    toast_fg "#141418"
    toast_bg "#ff5fd7"
}

// ── keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Everything is `prefix` then a key. Pressing the prefix twice sends the
// prefix itself to the pane. Modifiers are ctrl+ alt+ shift+ super+, and named
// keys are left/right/up/down/enter/tab/escape/space/backspace/home/end/
// pageup/pagedown/delete/insert/backslash/minus/slash/comma/period.
//
// Actions: split-cols split-rows close-pane focus-left focus-right focus-up
// focus-down focus-next resize-left resize-right resize-up resize-down equalize
// rotate-layout
// scroll-up scroll-down scroll-page-up scroll-page-down scroll-top
// scroll-bottom zoom minimize rerun new-tab next-tab prev-tab select-tab-1..9 finder
// palette help detach quit
// literal-prefix none

// What C-a e opens this file with. Empty means $EDITOR, and then vi -- the
// one editor a POSIX system is required to have, so a wrong guess you can see
// beats a pane that opens empty.
//
// The pane it opens is *ephemeral*: quit the editor and the pane goes, with
// no corpse to dismiss and no trace in a dumped layout. You asked for an
// editor, not for a record of having had one.
//
// editor "nvim"

keys {
    // The leader. Every binding below is this key, then the key on the line.
    // ctrl+a is a default and not a decision -- it is start-of-line to half
    // the world, and if that is you, change it here and nothing else: the
    // badge in the status bar and the cheatsheet (C-a ?) both name whatever
    // is bound, and the old key goes straight through to your shell.
    //
    // Pressing it twice always sends the key itself, whichever key it is.
    prefix "ctrl+a"

    bind "\\" "split-cols"
    bind "-" "split-rows"
    bind "x" "close-pane"
    // Run a dead pane's command again, in the same pane and on top of the
    // same scrollback. Refused on a pane that is still running.
    bind "r" "rerun"
    bind "z" "zoom"
    bind "m" "minimize"

    bind "h" "focus-left"
    bind "l" "focus-right"
    bind "k" "focus-up"
    bind "j" "focus-down"
    bind "left" "focus-left"
    bind "right" "focus-right"
    bind "up" "focus-up"
    bind "down" "focus-down"
    bind "o" "focus-next"

    // The boundary moves in the direction you press, whichever side of it the
    // focused pane is on.
    bind "shift+h" "resize-left"
    bind "shift+l" "resize-right"
    bind "shift+k" "resize-up"
    bind "shift+j" "resize-down"

    // Undo every drag and nudge at once: each split divided between the panes
    // behind it, so every visible pane gets an even share of the rows and
    // columns it competes for.
    bind "=" "equalize"

    // A quarter turn of the whole tab, clockwise: every split changes axis and
    // a row split's children reverse, because the pane on top of a stack is the
    // pane on the right once you turn it. Four presses come back exactly, so
    // there is nothing to undo -- which is what earns it the space bar, the
    // easiest key there is and the one tmux spends on cycling layouts. Refused
    // with a word when the turn would leave a pane under the floor: a share of
    // the height is not always a share the width can afford, and `=` first
    // usually fixes that.
    bind "space" "rotate-layout"

    bind "c" "new-tab"
    // Tab cycles tabs, the way every other tabbed thing does. `n` and `p`
    // used to, and are free again: `p` is worth more as the palette, which is
    // pressed rather more often than "the tab before this one".
    bind "tab" "next-tab"
    bind "shift+tab" "prev-tab"
    bind "1" "select-tab-1"
    bind "2" "select-tab-2"
    bind "3" "select-tab-3"

    bind "f" "finder"

    // Every action by name, including the ones nothing is bound to -- which
    // is the whole point of it, since an unbound action has no other way in.
    bind "p" "palette"

    // The cheatsheet, built from whatever is bound below rather than from a
    // list of what the defaults were -- rebind something and it says so.
    // Bound twice because a terminal may or may not tell us `?` was shifted.
    bind "?" "help"
    bind "/" "help"

    // Scrollback. The wheel does this too, unless the program in the pane
    // wants the mouse itself, or is a full-screen program with no scrollback
    // of ours to show — then the wheel means arrow keys to it.
    bind "pageup" "scroll-page-up"
    bind "pagedown" "scroll-page-down"
    bind "home" "scroll-top"
    bind "end" "scroll-bottom"
    bind "d" "detach"
    bind "q" "quit"

    // ── bindings that need no leader ────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Anything in here fires the moment you press it, with no prefix first.
    // That also means the chord is *gone* from every program in every pane:
    // vim will never see it, your shell will never see it, and the only sign
    // will be that the key does not work in there any more.
    //
    // It is a footgun and it is yours: this is your keyboard, and a
    // multiplexer that refuses to get out of the way is its own kind of
    // wrong. Prefer chords no program wants -- ctrl+alt+something is usually
    // safe, a bare letter certainly is not.
    //
    // `none` gives a chord back, the same as anywhere else.
    //
    // direct {
    //     bind "ctrl+alt+left" "focus-left"
    //     bind "ctrl+alt+right" "focus-right"
    //     bind "ctrl+alt+up" "focus-up"
    //     bind "ctrl+alt+down" "focus-down"
    // }
}
